[{:db/id 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a565f00-30b9-57be-9036-7aadde82bfe8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124361 :question/problem "Given that ![](images/25/25c8dd8e95095754a68305847645f68c6e872e9049f5062177bbac4d2fcd1164.svg) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ad99eb94-2179-5889-bd75-4a31770a9b01"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8f9a6384-39b0-554b-abeb-cd550f81dcbb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124345 :question/problem "Given that ![](images/2e/2e4a7f95853bbfc023f9f4be8278063ed945889e84fd6b2bc2100a22dbd90994.svg) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124347 :question/problem "Given that ![](images/bd/bdf92550ba4b8fa58da146c944a99a57e35e13e4885c3a2309ab291a4a4177e4.svg) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\:\\:x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^2-1\\:$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{12}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351500 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-28+15 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a565f00-30b9-57be-9036-7aadde82bfe8" :question/id #uuid "4a565f00-30b9-57be-9036-7aadde82bfe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-85922" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=4-2=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6843fb7-59e5-5002-ac90-2b5e6f7257b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6843fb7-59e5-5002-ac90-2b5e6f7257b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6843fb7-59e5-5002-ac90-2b5e6f7257b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2683b79-0149-589d-9aee-43b4a2b94124" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc2cc7ac-9a1b-526d-80a8-a3d8c973934c" "answer-4571d412-17c6-50e3-a5ad-250aba5664ee" "answer-8600afc6-a04f-52fd-8a1f-de56606615bb" "answer-f2683b79-0149-589d-9aee-43b4a2b94124" "answer-1d65a687-0988-5518-994a-fcab04da4ebc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc2cc7ac-9a1b-526d-80a8-a3d8c973934c" :answer/id #uuid "cc2cc7ac-9a1b-526d-80a8-a3d8c973934c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4571d412-17c6-50e3-a5ad-250aba5664ee" :answer/id #uuid "4571d412-17c6-50e3-a5ad-250aba5664ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8600afc6-a04f-52fd-8a1f-de56606615bb" :answer/id #uuid "8600afc6-a04f-52fd-8a1f-de56606615bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2683b79-0149-589d-9aee-43b4a2b94124" :answer/id #uuid "f2683b79-0149-589d-9aee-43b4a2b94124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d65a687-0988-5518-994a-fcab04da4ebc" :answer/id #uuid "1d65a687-0988-5518-994a-fcab04da4ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351499 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=5-6=-1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125141 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -2 \\\\ 71 & 10\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-14 & -2 \\\\ 71 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-14)\\cdot 10-(-2)\\cdot 71 \\\\  & =-140+142 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123446 :answer-field/correct 17592186123447 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354442 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-3)-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-15-10 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad99eb94-2179-5889-bd75-4a31770a9b01" :question/id #uuid "ad99eb94-2179-5889-bd75-4a31770a9b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-83394" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=5$ and $\\text{det}(A)=9.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =5A-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa304203-2130-5c26-be53-cf5953cd34e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa304203-2130-5c26-be53-cf5953cd34e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa304203-2130-5c26-be53-cf5953cd34e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f30920a8-926e-571d-b2ce-067087fbb78e" "answer-3bde7c58-3f7c-5801-a261-da5680905d36" "answer-5d2122a1-0044-5550-a7b5-8c659f887082" "answer-dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" "answer-8506e219-a01e-50d0-9da2-c59e5aa39951"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f30920a8-926e-571d-b2ce-067087fbb78e" :answer/id #uuid "f30920a8-926e-571d-b2ce-067087fbb78e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5A-9I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bde7c58-3f7c-5801-a261-da5680905d36" :answer/id #uuid "3bde7c58-3f7c-5801-a261-da5680905d36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9A+5I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d2122a1-0044-5550-a7b5-8c659f887082" :answer/id #uuid "5d2122a1-0044-5550-a7b5-8c659f887082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5A+9I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" :answer/id #uuid "dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5A-9I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8506e219-a01e-50d0-9da2-c59e5aa39951" :answer/id #uuid "8506e219-a01e-50d0-9da2-c59e5aa39951" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9A-5I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f9a6384-39b0-554b-abeb-cd550f81dcbb" :question/id #uuid "8f9a6384-39b0-554b-abeb-cd550f81dcbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-85930" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-3-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+2) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+5\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{A}^{3}+5A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}(-{A}^{3}+5A-2I) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{2}+5I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =5I-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34db680c-e9af-57f5-a298-893f9c03dc9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34db680c-e9af-57f5-a298-893f9c03dc9c" :answer-field/id #uuid "34db680c-e9af-57f5-a298-893f9c03dc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-054199c2-5c89-5ffd-aead-8b50d8e9a898" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97594f4f-bc35-5637-b1b2-7a025224b4ca" "answer-8e1bad79-e653-5c1b-8674-be7f593dc2d9" "answer-87762782-8da5-5b58-aec6-89ebd72a1767" "answer-10181856-84b4-5e5e-8832-61fff9b19f1a" "answer-054199c2-5c89-5ffd-aead-8b50d8e9a898"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97594f4f-bc35-5637-b1b2-7a025224b4ca" :answer/id #uuid "97594f4f-bc35-5637-b1b2-7a025224b4ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e1bad79-e653-5c1b-8674-be7f593dc2d9" :answer/id #uuid "8e1bad79-e653-5c1b-8674-be7f593dc2d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}I-\\frac{5}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87762782-8da5-5b58-aec6-89ebd72a1767" :answer/id #uuid "87762782-8da5-5b58-aec6-89ebd72a1767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10181856-84b4-5e5e-8832-61fff9b19f1a" :answer/id #uuid "10181856-84b4-5e5e-8832-61fff9b19f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-054199c2-5c89-5ffd-aead-8b50d8e9a898" :answer/id #uuid "054199c2-5c89-5ffd-aead-8b50d8e9a898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125794 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of a square matrix $A$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the left-hand side of this equation, we have the expression \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I),\n$$\n\n which we call the **characteristic polynomial** of the matrix $A.$\n\nFor instance, let's find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix ![](images/1d/1d6d0bdedbb25bbc41759e443cdc0f6a207ef5cbfa4761b05e1a1a2eda24cbec.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(C-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =(-8-\\lambda )\\cdot (7-\\lambda )-(-9)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125795 :tutorial/content "The **Cayley-Hamilton theorem** states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $\\,n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix whose characteristic polynomial is* \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) = (-1)^n\\lambda^n + c_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1\\lambda + c_0.\n$$\n\n*Then, the matrix $A$ is a solution of the characteristic equation $p(\\lambda) = 0$.* \n\nThat is to say, if we substitute $A$ into the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$, taking $A^0 = I,$ then we have\n\n$$\np(A) = (-1)^nA^n + c_{n-1}A^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1A + c_0I = O,\n$$\n\nwhere $O$ is the zero-matrix. \n\nFor example, according to this theorem, for the matrix \n![](images/d2/d24f480293267a9ddc28b03626e7bde6f01bd62a136fed17f8580bf1ea34d776.svg)\nwhose characteristic polynomial is \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2 +\\lambda -2,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 +A -2I =O.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125824 :tutorial/content "If we want to evaluate a high-degree polynomial for a given matrix input, it can take a lot of time. For example, suppose we wanted to evaluate the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^{10} - 5x^9 - 2x^8 + x + 2\n$$\n\nfor the input ![](images/0f/0f9bff826261a956305962b4ca945ed91012cc5042e6ffe92a4b5f1ce13d3237.svg) Computing the $10$th power of this matrix would be a lot of work!\n\nLuckily, there is a way to speed up the process. The trick is to use Cayley-Hamilton theorem, which tells us that every matrix is a solution to its characteristic equation.\n\nFor the given matrix ![](images/65/6514096b22aed47005e5ff59c47e3cec701aed5b120b0022b186f1bec941d953.svg) the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the Cayley-Hamilton theorem tells us that\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 - 5A - 2I = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, observe that the polynomial $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{10}-5{x}^{9}-2{x}^{8}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}({x}^{2}-5x-2)+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}p(x)+x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = A^8 p(A) + A + 2I,\n$$\n\nand because $p(A) = 0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{8}\\cdot 0+A+2I \\\\  & =0+A+2I \\\\  & =A+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the computation is much faster:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =A+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it may not be so obvious how to group the terms of $f(x)$ to reveal $p(x).$ In the general case, it's best to divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division, and use the result\n\n$$\nf(x) = q(x) p(x) + r(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder. Let's see an example of this." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124757 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
